winerenku.drv: mouse pointer shapes (SetCursor). Not in the patchset; kept as a starting point.

Left out by choice: over Wine windows the pointer stays RenkuOS's own arrow, which is more
coherent than one that changes with every program. Use this only if a program needs its
cursor, for example a plug-in that hides the pointer while a knob is dragged.

What it does: RENKU_SetCursor (new cursor.c) shows Win32's standard cursors (user32 OCR_*,
comctl32 IDC_COPY/IDC_DIVIDER*, ole32 drag cursors) as app_server's built-in BCursorID shapes,
and draws any other cursor from its bitmaps as a premultiplied B_RGBA32 BCursor (app_server
expects premultiplied colour, HWInterface::_DrawCursor). A hidden cursor becomes
B_CURSOR_ID_NO_CURSOR. The cursor is set on the top-level window's BView with SetViewCursor.

State: builds cleanly on the RenkuOS build VM, but does not work yet. In its only test,
win32u did call it and it chose the I-beam over notepad's edit area (+cursor trace), yet the
pointer on screen did not change. The cause was not looked into. Not done either: animated
cursors (first frame only), SetCursorPos, ClipCursor.

Base: the wine-11.21 dev tree with patches 1-18 (tree 21a34855). To use it there:
  git apply attic/winerenku-cursor-shapes.diff, then commit it as a patch of its own.

